# Jenkins pipelines

Edit `Jenkinsfile` / pipeline entrypoints as **portable CI definitions**.
Match the repository’s existing declarative or scripted style. Do not invent
a second Pipeline dialect or hard-code a product’s private job graph.

Also follow `jenkins-ci-from-main` for SCM pickup reminders.

## Prefer declarative when the repo already does

```groovy
pipeline {
  agent any
  options {
    timestamps()
  }
  stages {
    stage('Check') {
      steps {
        // match existing checkout / test patterns in this repo
      }
    }
  }
  post {
    always {
      cleanWs()
    }
  }
}
```

- Keep stage names stable when dashboards or downstream jobs key on them
- Prefer existing shared libraries / credentials bindings already used in
  neighboring Jenkinsfiles
- YAML pipeline entrypoints also follow `code-yaml`

## Agnostic content

- No inline passwords, tokens, or private keys — use Jenkins credentials ids
  the job already references, or host-approved secret stores
- Do not hard-code machine-specific absolute paths unless the repo standard
  already does
- Parameterized jobs: declare parameters explicitly; document defaults in
  the Jenkinsfile when the project expects that

## Safety

- Avoid `checkout scm` replacements that widen trust without being asked
- Do not disable `sandbox` or add `MethodPermission` exceptions unless the
  user explicitly requires it and peers already do
- Destructive `post` / cleanup must not wipe unrelated workspaces

## Verify

After editing, confirm with a build that checks out the new commit
(`jenkins-builds`). Do not claim the pipeline is fixed until that evidence
exists.
